unction of one or more of  these subsets looking at the pathway of  development it's easy to note that genes  that are involved in the progenitor  stage can involve multiple cell types  where as genes that are specific to a  t-cell or B cells if mutations occur in  these genes then those particular  subsets may be affected and again the  genes in the earlier part of development  may result in a lack of these cell types  in the periphery whereas genes involved  in the later part of development as in  differentiation of the T cell into cd4  or cd8 may result in a functional  impairment of these cells  so how does immune-deficiency present  now the Jeffrey model foundation that  has been involved in advocacy for  primary immunodeficiency has these  posters that are that you may find when  traveling around the country and doctors  offices and they are used very heavily  to educate patients on the signs of  immune deficiency so let's take a look  at the ten signs of immune deficiency  the 10th  sorry clinical signs and symptoms that  should point towards immune deficiency  from the poster on the left-hand side of  the slide we see that if the patient  presents with four or more new year  infections within a year two or more  serious sinus infections within a year  two or more months on antibiotics with  little effect two or more pneumonias  within a year a failure of an infant to  gain weight or to grow normally  recomment deep skin or organ abscesses  persistent thrush which is Candida  infection in the mouth or fungal  infection on the skin the need for  intravenous antibiotics to clear  infections or two or more deep-seated  infections including septicemia and also  a family history of primary  immunodeficiency so patients presenting  with any of these warning signs would  warrant further investigation into their  immune system to determine if there are  any abnormalities that might point  towards primary immunodeficiency the  poster on the right presents this  information in a more child-friendly  manner because as we are aware primary  immunodeficiency we tend to find quite  commonly in pediatric patients so the  same information is presented in a  series of cartoons making it easier to  explain them to our pediatric patients  now having said that primary  immunodeficiency manifest in the  pediatric age group it's always been  considered that these are fairly rare  immune deficiencies these are fairly  rare clinical conditions however over  the course of the years we have found  that primary immunodeficiency is  probably more common than previously  thought so this slide is taken from a  paper published in 2014 and it shows the  ten year trend of patients that were  followed in clinics that were identified  with primary immunodeficiencies patients  who were referred to centers where  patients with immune deficiencies are  treated or patients receiving  intravenous immunoglobulin and as you  can see here in all of the graphs in  2004 these numbers were fairly low in  the 5,000 and as you can see over the  years there are certainly larger numbers  of patients who are being treated  identified and followed and it's not  because the incidence of primary  immunodeficiency is increasing it's  because we have better tools to  recognize them both clinically and  within the laboratory so the little  cartoon that you see up here on the  right that says think zebra which has  been used by the immune deficiency  foundation is really to say you know  formerly in medical school we're all  taught that when you hear hoofbeats  think of horses or think of the most  common clinical condition the likeliest  possibility however for immune  deficiency it's important to say well  when you see a patient with chronic  infections perhaps we should be thinking  of zebras think about immune deficiency  disorders that though they may be rare  are being recognized increasingly just  because we are now have we now have a  better sense of  volcanium deficie