utations  can be inherited in an autosomal  recessive manner causing perhaps miler  disease and can be seen in  males or females so the clinical  presentation of x-linked chronic  granulomatous disease is in males they  present with severe bacterial and fungal  infections within the first two years of  life bacterial abscesses very severe  skin and gastrointestinal tract  infections the autosomal recessive form  is males or females they may have milder  infections really depending on the  extent of the oxidative burst activity  and finally it's important to note that  with the x-linked disease females are  carriers and they may be asymptomatic  all those newer publications indicate  that they may present with some  autoimmune manifestations  so the laboratory diagnosis of CJD is  again done in a number of labs around  the country and is done with a fairly  simple test in whole blood so in this  assay whole blood is stimulated with PMA  and these cells are preloaded with a dye  called dihydrate amine one two three now  this dye once the loaded into the  neutrophils and the neutrophils are  stimulated can fluoresce very brightly  because it gets oxidized by the reactive  oxygen intermediates so shown in this  flow plot on the left hand side are  neutrophils identified by the scatter  gate the second plot shows us the  neutrophils with no dye the middle plot  is DHR loaded into the neutrophils  showing baseline fluorescence and  finally the plot on the far right shows  us the fluorescence of DHR once the  cells are stimulated and as you can see  the fluorescence is off scale here  because it is so bright  following stimulation of normal  neutrophils so we calculate an index  value that is based on the fluorescence  of the stimulated cells divided by the  fluorescence  of the unstimulated cells and each lab  establishes their own index value in our  lab it is greater than 30 so an index  value of greater than 30 indicates a  normal oxidative burst if it's less than  that then this is indicated of a defect  in oxidative burst and needs to be  investigated further so let's take a  look at some plots published in 2017 for  chronic granulomatous disease on the  left hand side we see the top histograms  showing a normal response the resting  cells show very little activity the  simulated cells show a nice peak once  oxidative burst occurs x-linked chronic  granulomatous disease in the middle from  a baby boy show a complete lack of  oxidative burst the carriers phenotype  is shown in the plots below so the plot  on the left-hand side shows no activity  and then there are two peaks following  stimulation indicating an abnormal at a  normal neutrophil population autosomal  recessive patients are rather more  difficult to diagnose simply because  there's more variation in their  oxidative burst result so shown here on  the right hand side are some plots from  autosomal recessive CJD patients one of  the characteristics is a rather broad  peak shown in the top plots on the right  hand side following stimulation so  instead of seeing a nice sharp peak upon  stimulation these patients may show a  rather broader flatter peak indicating  perhaps compromised oxidative burst it's  also interesting to note that one of the  patients in this publication showed no  oxidative burst whatsoever but were  later confirmed to be autosomal  recessive when you look at the data they  look like a classic x-linked CJD so  again it emphasizes that while the  protein  and functional studies are important  genetic sequencing is always important  to confirm the diagnosis of primary  immunodeficiency so I'd like to leave  you with some final thoughts this is a  very extensive topic and I have covered  a high level of primary  immunodeficiencies and gone over some of  the more commonly used diagnostic  testing diagnosis of primary  immunodeficiencies does pose challenges  both to the physician and laboratory  it may not follow a linear path we may  not be able to just go from t BN k  analyses to b cell phenotype or t cell  phenotype and function these immune  deficiencies may present in a very  complex way making it difficult to  choose any one clinical test for an  obvious clinical presentation like  chronic granulomatous disease it may be  easy enough to take the clinical  presentation and go straight away to  neutrophil oxidative burst laboratory  analysis the phenotype and function may  be followed by genetic analysis when  required and finally I'd like to leave  you with the thought that diagnosis of  immune deficiency can be complicated  because it may be influenced by other  factors for the types of infections that  the patient has may in turn impact  immune function the genetics the  epigenetics the environment what are  they exposed to so it's very important  to get a good combination of clinical  history family history clinical  examination and careful laboratory  evaluation to reach a diagnosis I would  like to acknowledge both of the  institutions that I have worked at in  the past ten years the Children's  Hospital Colorado where I am currently  with the Department of Pathology  laboratory medicine flow cytometry lab  as well as the section of clinical  immunology and allergy within the  department of pediatrics  and at national Jewish Health where I  was previously with the advanced  diagnostics immunology and flow  cytometry lab and the division of  clinical immunology and Allergy  Department of Pediatrics as my  collaborators and of course a lot of  what I have discussed today would not be  possible without the collaboration with  the physicians and particularly the  patients at both of these institutions  much of what we have developed over the  years is all because of patients coming  in with these unusual presentations and  requiring us to think beyond standard  immunological testing and think about  how best to go beyond that to more  extensive phenotyping and function so  thank you very much for listening in and  I'm happy to take any questions  thank you dr. night for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left to your screen  and we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for so let's  get started our first question is if  adequate numbers of t-cells are detected  by flow cytometry in a patient's  peripheral blood sample does it rule out  immune deficiency Thank You Alexis that  is a really important question one of  our the first line of diagnosis  laboratory diagnostic tests is the TB NK  analysis which detects T cells B cells  and NK cells as we've seen in the  presentation when we take the example of  a failed treck screen and analyze a baby  sample as follow-up we may actually  detect T cells so does it necessarily  rule out immune deficiency not  necessarily because these T cells could  be a maternal origin they could be  memory t-cells they could be  functionally impaired some of the genes  involved in T cell development do not  impact T cell development but impact T  cell function so when we are  suspecting immune-deficiency very  strongly from a clinical perspective and  even if we do detect adequate numbers of  t-cells if the suspicion is high enough  we should move on to further phenotyping  them to see if they are naive or memory  or subsets of memory t-cells and are  they functional if we stimulate them  will they proliferate in the laboratory  setting so follow-up testing is still  very important  now dr. Knight our next question is a  failed newborn screen for skin  necessarily indicative of a genetic  defect so a failed newborn screen for  skin indicates that there are low  numbers of t-cells it is important to  follow up with flow cytometry to  determine if they truly are low numbers  of t-cells it's not always necessary  that these babies that fail the skin  screen have a genetic defect sometimes  it could be due to loss of lymph fluid  lymphatic leakage so the T cells are not  circulating in the periphery but they  are being generated in normal numbers a  failed skid screen may be commonly seen  in premature babies so it's important to  understand the other clinical features  before going on to genetics in case of a  premature baby testing may be warranted  in a few weeks to allow for the immune  system to mature and then repeat the  test again again another example that we  touched upon is the DiGeorge syndrome  where the thymus may not have developed  adequately or is absent so again t-cells  are being produced normally this is not  a genetic defect of t-cell development  but it is instead a problem with the  thymus so not all failed skin screens  necessarily indicate a genetic defect  and it looks like we have time for one  more question  when you do an immune assessment of a  patient you mentioned running CBC  immunoglobulin and a complement in  addition to the T and B cell uation can  you comment on whether you order all of  test at the same time or if results are  some dictate for the testing that's a  great question Alexis and yes so we  normally go with an initial screen a  complete blood cell count is almost  routine when you're examining a patient  with immune deficiency just to get a  sense of numbers of lymphocytes  monocytes and so on again serum  immunoglobulins is a good indicator but  again we often go with the clinical  picture now if the patient has severe  infection say with capsulated bacterial  pneumococcal infections we might lean  more towards looking at the  immunoglobulins and complement because  both of those components are really  important for protection against  capsulated bacteria if the patient has  say severe fungal infections or viral  infections we might lean more towards  analyzing the t cell arm of the immune  system so while this broad screen is  helpful I think in many cases in the I  would say the community setting when  you're thinking about immune deficiency  a CBC immunoglobulins TB NK at the very  least are very useful to get a global  assessment of the immune system if  there's a strong suspicion or clinical  history of encapsulated bacterial  infections and complement testing could  be added in since it is a critical  component  thank you dr. nate do you have any final  comments for audience I would just like  to say that it's been a pleasure of  developing and putting this webinar  together and thank you to both lab roots  and binding site for hosting it this  primary immunodeficiency I would say is  an area that is continually growing new  defects are continuously being  identified the laboratory plays a really  important role in working  collaboratively with the physician  in helping to analyze diagnose and  follow these patients so again thank you  fall for listening in and I hope that  you have found this webinar useful thank  you again dr. night for your time today  and your tan research we would also like  to think levers and our sponsor binding  site for underwriting today's  educational webcast before we go I'd  like to thank the audience for joining  us today and for their interesting  questions question we did not have time  for today and those submitted during the  on demand period will be addressed by  the speaker via the contact information  that you provided at the time of  registration this webcast can be viewed  on-demand through May of 2019 leverage  will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

